# Santali in the Ol Chiki script (U+1C50-U+1C7F). espeak-ng has no Santali
# voice and reads Ol Chiki letters as their code points under every voice
# ("letter 1c65"), so there is no reference reading to be compatible with:
# both modes give this file's reading. Written for uccharan from these
# descriptions:
#   Michael Everson (2005), "Final proposal to encode the Ol Chiki script in
#     the UCS", ISO/IEC JTC1/SC2/WG2 N2984R = Unicode L2/05-243R, after Zide
#     (1996) and Raghunath Murmu's Ranar: the letter values; ᱹ (gaahlaa
#     ttuddaag) after ᱟ ᱮ ᱚ writes the Santal Parganas vowels ə ɛ ɔ; ᱸ (mu
#     ttuddag) nasalises a vowel, ᱺ (mu-gaahlaa ttuddaag) is the two
#     together, ᱻ (relaa) lengthens a vowel; the four "glottal" letters ᱜ ᱡ
#     ᱫ ᱵ are checked stops word-finally and voiced stops before a vowel,
#     ᱼ (phaarkaa) keeps the checked value before a vowel, ᱽ (ahad) gives
#     the voiced value where the checked one would be read; ᱷ after a
#     consonant writes aspiration;
#   Arun Ghosh (2008), "Santali", in G. D. S. Anderson (ed.), The Munda
#     Languages (Routledge), pp. 11-98, and Lukas Neukom (2001), Santali
#     (LINCOM), as summarised in the Wikipedia article "Santali language":
#     the phonemes (vowels i e ɛ ə a ɔ o u, nasal vowels; p t ʈ c k ʔ, b d
#     ɖ ɟ ɡ, aspirates mostly in loanwords, m n ɳ ɲ ŋ, s h, r ɽ, l, w j);
#     word-final stops of native words are checked: glottalised and
#     unreleased (Sebeok 1943 and Ghosh 2008 treat them as allophones of
#     p t c k), written here k̚ʔ c̚ʔ t̚ʔ p̚ʔ;
#   Gregory D. S. Anderson and Luke Horo (2021), "Towards a prosodic
#     typology of the Kherwarian languages: Santali of Assam", JSEALS
#     Special Publication 8: Santali is strongly second-syllable prominent
#     in uninflected disyllables (primary stress on the second syllable).
# Choices made here where the sources leave it open: a glottal letter before
# a voiceless consonant (ᱢᱤᱫᱴᱟᱹᱝ, ᱦᱤᱥᱟᱹᱵᱛᱮ) is checked like a final one,
# before a voiced consonant (ᱜᱤᱫᱨᱟᱹ, ᱰᱤᱜᱨᱤ) it is voiced; ᱚᱹ, whose contrast
# with ᱚ is marginal, is ɔ; ᱭ ᱣ ᱶ after a vowel and before a consonant or the
# end of the word are the second half of a diphthong (i u ũ), ᱶ before a
# vowel is the nasal glide w̃; ᱦ is h; ᱻ after a glottal letter is a mistyped
# ᱼ (IndicCorp v2 ᱢᱮᱱᱟᱜᱻᱟ); marks found after other letters are not said.
# Vowel harmony and the dialect differences in the vowels (Ghosh 2008) are
# not written in Ol Chiki and are not modelled: every letter keeps its value.

